  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will ridge to the southwest across the\nplains, resulting in seasonal temperatures for southwest Kansas\ndespite the upper level ridging. Southwest winds will develop \nthis morning in response to a surface trough, with increasing \nspeeds to 10-15 mph by late morning. An upper level trough \ntraversing the northern plains will push a weak cold front \nthrough Kansas after midnight, with winds becoming northerly at \n10-12 kts. Despite the frontal passage, temperatures are \nforecast to rise into the 70s and stay in that range through \nSunday and Monday. These readings are near the climatological \naverage. \n\nHowever, the mid to upper level flow is forecast to become more\nzonal next week, allowing for weak downslope flow and warmer \nthan average temperatures. Highs are forecast to be in the 80s \neach day Tuesday through Friday. Winds are expected to be light,\nthrough the forecast period, with the exception of Monday when \n10-20 mph southwest winds start to advect warmer air into \nKansas.\n\nWith the lack of any upper level trough across the western USA,\ndry weather will persist for the next week to ten days given the\nlack of low to mid level moisture and forcing mechanisms. The \nvarious ensemble suites show measurable rain chances near 0% \nthrough all of next week.",
